SARAH NOVOTNY: Flattery.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:32.730
Flattery, yes.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:33.160
MARK MANDEL: Indeed.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.270
MELANIE WARRICK:
We'll take that.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:35.144
MARK MANDEL: So yeah.

00:06:35.144 --> 00:06:38.040
You're the head of open source
strategy at Google Cloud

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.130
Platform.

00:06:39.130 --> 00:06:40.180
What does that mean?

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.430
What do you do with your time?

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.010
SARAH NOVOTNY: So let me tell
you a little bit about how

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:47.220
I got here inside
Google, because that

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.530
leads into the story of,
what does this group do?

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.060
Because this is a new group.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:54.720
So I was hired into Google to
embed myself in the Kubernetes

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.800
community, and turn it from
being a company led project

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.540
to a community led project.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:05.460
We knew that as the
player in cloud,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.250
we needed to have a way to
change the portable workload

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:10.110
unit story for cloud.

00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:11.090
It couldn't be VMs.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.020
It couldn't be Java, JAR files.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.900
We wanted to move this
along with the work

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.420
that we had done here at
Google with containers.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.420
And containers have been the
backbone of Google's workloads

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.410
for 15 years.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.360
And we did a lot of that work in
open source, in Linux early on,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.882
and brought it out to the
world and made that possible.

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:31.590
But we needed to really
change the story,

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:35.070
because the story was very
much about VMs and cloud

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.250
at that point.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.730
And so Google made this huge
investment in Kubernetes,

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:40.380
and built a team.

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:44.340
And the strategic goal was to
change how the industry talked

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:45.840
about portable workloads units.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.530
And we needed to do that in
a way that allowed Kubernetes

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.451
to be ubiquitous.

00:07:51.451 --> 00:07:52.950
That was the first
goal is we needed

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:54.330
Kubernetes to be everywhere.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.610
We needed containers
to be everywhere.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:07:59.670
And in order to do that, you
have to build a huge community.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.560
And open source seemed a
really obvious way to do that.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:04.814
It's a much easier way
than a single product,

00:08:04.814 --> 00:08:06.480
because you get a lot
of people's input.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:07.660
You get a lot of engagement.

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:10.540
You get other people advocating
for you and with you.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:13.770
But you also can't then
have one company leading it.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:17.110
So I was brought in to
help make that transition.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:19.980
And then that turned
into sort of, oh,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.660
and I'm doing an
open source project.

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.230
Can you help me
with my community?

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:24.980
And, oh, I'm doing an
open source project.

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:27.060
And then we open
sourced TensorFlow.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:28.830
And we had different
strategic goals,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.560
which then meant, how do we
deal with a community that

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.810
has different strategic goals?

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.929
And we've done open
source for years.

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:38.130
There's lots and lots
of open source work

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.980
that Google has done,
going back to the inception

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:41.740
of the company.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:45.960
But it was usually, early
on, a scratch your own itch,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:46.920
and then upstream it.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.010
Make sure that we are
being good citizens, being

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.540
good open source citizens.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.270
And then there was, of
course, Chrome and Android,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:57.174
which were open source projects,
but they were very company led.

00:08:57.174 --> 00:08:58.590
And the communities
were much more

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:01.880
about ecosystems, and
applications, and the adoption,

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:05.720
and ways to extend the
particular project.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:07.630
But Kubernetes had
to be different.

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:09.750
And so we've seen all
these different strategies.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.810
And now we're collecting a group
for Google Cloud Platform that

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:15.960
sits, embeds in communities
that are either first party

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:18.390
communities, communities
that Google has built

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:22.320
and wants to continue to grow,
or third party communities,

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.120
like Node.js.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.410
I've recently joined the
Node.js foundation board

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.350
in order to help
that project become

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:32.760
more appealing to
enterprises, grow through sort

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:35.580
of the early adopter
phase, cross the chasm,

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.040
and get into the
enterprise adoption phase,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.120
and grow as a project, make
themselves more long term

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.290
sustainable.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.230
So my team covers a
whole pile of projects,

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:51.420
TensorFlow, Kubernetes, GRPC,
[? Isdio, ?] Apache Beam.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:52.932
I know I'm forgetting something.

00:09:52.932 --> 00:09:54.390
MARK MANDEL: Do
you do open census?

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:55.710
SARAH NOVOTNY:
Actually, we don't yet.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:56.950
But we have consulted with them.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:58.700
Which leads to the
next thing, because you

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:01.770
were going to ask me, what
does a day look like in this?

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.510
It's actually a lot
of meetings, sadly.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.920
A lot of foundation meetings.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.140
A lot of internal meetings,
finding new projects

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:09.434
that people are
building, and finding

00:10:09.434 --> 00:10:11.100
what their strategic
goals are for them.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.890
So trying to make
sure we understand,

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.690
or like you consulting me
about your gaming project.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:17.500
MARK MANDEL: I was just
thinking about that.

00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:18.400
SARAH NOVOTNY: Yeah.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.660
That looked like that.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.076
How do we achieve the goals
that we have for this project?

00:10:22.076 --> 00:10:24.560
Is the project an interesting
way to showcase GCP?

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:25.060
OK.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:26.650
That's one reason
we might have it.

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:29.590
Is it an interesting way
to get into a community

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:32.380
or an academic space,
and get to know

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:33.880
all the players in that space?

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.100
Are we trying to
recruit with it?

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.130
Are we trying to make talent
more portable as well?

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.110
Are we trying to make
workloads more portable?

00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:43.193
So we look at what the
strategies are, and then we

00:10:43.193 --> 00:10:44.620
try to build
communities that work

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.590
to engage with those
strategies, and deliver

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:49.220
good economic
benefit for Google.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.500
Because while open source is
awesome and altruism is great,

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:56.420
we still are a business,
and I still pay rent.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.545
So I would like to have
the business be successful.

00:10:58.545 --> 00:11:00.003
MELANIE WARRICK:
When I was working

00:11:00.003 --> 00:11:01.540
in open source, the
common question,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.706
like everyone who would
meet me would be like, well,

00:11:03.706 --> 00:11:04.850
how do you guys make money?

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:06.384
Not that that's
my question here.

00:11:06.384 --> 00:11:07.800
SARAH NOVOTNY: I
was going to say,

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.100
I can answer that
for some of them.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:10.433
MELANIE WARRICK: Actually, yeah.

00:11:10.433 --> 00:11:11.000
Sure.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:12.950
How do some of these
projects make money?

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:14.230
SARAH NOVOTNY: Well, the
interesting thing is, though,

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.810
when we talk to these projects,
and what their strategy

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:18.310
is and why, one of
the things we ask

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:21.980
is, and how are we going
to monetize this or not?

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:23.540
If we're not going
to, that's fine.

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:24.670
But we need to
know that upfront,

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:26.628
because then it's part
of the success criteria.

00:11:26.628 --> 00:11:28.510
And for Kubernetes,
as an example,

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:33.040
we monetize it by having a
hosted version of the project.

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.390
We are one of many companies
that have a hosted version.

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:36.100
And that's fine.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:38.500
But we differentiate
based on integrations

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.990
with our other products,
or our network,

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.030
or all of these different
things that Google

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:44.860
can bring to bear as a company.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.740
For some of the other
open source projects,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:51.850
there are parallel versions
that are products primarily.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.010
Some of them also aren't yet.

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:55.240
And that's also great.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.560
Some of them are just, we
were doing this anyway.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:11:59.470
And we'd rather we had
an industry standard

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:01.810
and we led that
standard, as opposed

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:05.620
to being either locked in
by another person's standard

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:07.170
or locked out by their standard.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:08.420
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:11.361
I can see certain net benefits
across certain open source

00:12:11.361 --> 00:12:11.860
projects.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.310
I think with something
like open census,

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:15.310
and the vision that
I've seen, or the vision

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.297
that I can see from [INAUDIBLE]
our podcast of being

00:12:18.297 --> 00:12:20.380
able to have an integrated
toolchain that runs all

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.810
the way from GCP out into
the open source ecosystem

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:23.790
is very compelling.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.748
So it makes sense for us
to work in that space.

00:12:25.748 --> 00:12:27.640
SARAH NOVOTNY: Or
Forseti with security.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:29.080
RSA is happening this week.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:31.760
And the industry
needs these tools.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:33.830
And we sort of have
been doing them anyway.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:35.710
And it's not any
real secret sauce.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.582
So why don't we share them and
help drive the conversation?

00:12:38.582 --> 00:12:40.040
MARK MANDEL: Two
previous podcasts.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:40.990
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
definitely smart.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:41.260
Yes.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:41.820
We'll have to link those.

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:41.986
SARAH NOVOTNY: Oh.

00:12:41.986 --> 00:12:43.990
Were you talking Forseti
two previous podcasts ago?

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:44.110
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:45.090
We had Forseti on
a little while ago.

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:45.290
SARAH NOVOTNY: Nice.

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:45.789
See.

00:12:45.789 --> 00:12:48.430
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, and
also, prior to your podcast,

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:49.840
we had Eric Brewer.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:51.400
And we talked about
his involvement

00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:53.350
around getting
Kubernetes out there.

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:55.120
Did you guys work
together when you

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.580
came on and started leading up
the strategy around Kubernetes?

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:01.890
SARAH NOVOTNY: He actually
was even earlier than I was.

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:05.560
So I came in as we were
trying to make the shift

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:06.940
to move it to a foundation.

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.010
So I did a lot of the
early foundation work,

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.720
with Craig McLuckie.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.610
But Joe Beta had already
moved out of Google.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.130
He's still working
in Kubernetes.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.546
but Craig McLuckie
was still here.

00:13:16.546 --> 00:13:19.100
He's now since gone and
founded Heptio with Joe.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:20.680
Brandon Burns was still here.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:23.620
But Eric had been
sort of the strategy

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:26.470
at the very, very beginning,
and sort of the early documents,

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:27.850
and the early socializing of it.

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:29.650
But I wasn't interacting
with him a lot.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:30.650
MELANIE WARRICK: Got it.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:34.420
SARAH NOVOTNY: I have since
because we did a bunch of work

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:36.087
together last year
around Next actually.

00:13:36.087 --> 00:13:36.920
MELANIE WARRICK: Ah.

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:37.450
Nice.

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:38.470
MARK MANDEL: So you
mentioned something

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:39.670
I think interesting
there when you talk

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:40.670
about the foundation, right?

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:40.880
SARAH NOVOTNY: Yes.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:41.950
MARK MANDEL: CNCF.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:42.759
What is CNCF?

00:13:42.759 --> 00:13:43.550
What are its goals?

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:46.970
But also, what drives some
products to go to CNCF and some

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:47.470
don't?

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:48.930
And what's the impetus there?

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.070
SARAH NOVOTNY: Foundations 101.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:53.590
So the Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:56.260
was created to house Kubernetes.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:59.350
So the original
vision as Kubernetes

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:01.990
was being built inside
Google, even as we were just

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:04.870
having a couple of partners,
and just having trials with it,

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:07.900
and working out how we were
going to announce it, well

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:11.410
before I started, that would
have been late 2014, early

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:15.490
2015, there was a concept
that in order for this

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:19.060
to be much more trusted
by the industry,

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:20.950
it couldn't be just Google.

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:23.260
As I said, it had to be
really community led.

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.050
And Google has had
some challenges

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:28.180
with holding things too tightly.

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:30.640
And so we wanted to make
sure that it was clear

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:33.460
that Google was really
intentional about this.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:39.220
And so in July of 2015 when 1.0
of Kubernetes was announced,

00:14:39.220 --> 00:14:41.350
we announced, at the
same time, an intent

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:44.884
to build a foundation, and put
Kubernetes in the foundation.

00:14:44.884 --> 00:14:46.300
We had looked at
other foundations

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:48.786
and talked about whether
or not they would fit.

00:14:48.786 --> 00:14:50.410
But the Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:51.500
ended up being created.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:53.110
And we wanted it to
be a broader story

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:55.300
than just Kubernetes,
because Kubernetes

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:58.220
was the start of
the transformation.

00:14:58.220 --> 00:14:59.860
So the Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.520
looks at what it means
to build applications

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:06.250
that are built and architected
primarily for the cloud.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.460
So the architecture that
it was framed around

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.970
was container packaged,
dynamically scheduled,

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:15.600
and something else really
important I'm sure.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.691
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:17.691 --> 00:15:18.190
Ha.

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:19.410
Microservices oriented.

00:15:19.410 --> 00:15:20.274
There we go.

00:15:20.274 --> 00:15:21.190
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:15:21.190 --> 00:15:21.280
Nice one.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.300
SARAH NOVOTNY: I pulled it out.

00:15:22.300 --> 00:15:23.020
MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:15:23.020 --> 00:15:24.936
SARAH NOVOTNY: So that
was the framing of what

00:15:24.936 --> 00:15:26.190
it means to be cloud native.

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:28.990
And the cloud native compute
foundation is safe haven.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:32.380
So it's not company led
for any of these projects.

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:34.320
The idea is it's
a great place that

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:36.540
is independent of any
particular company or company

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:41.820
agenda for multiple industry
partners and multiple industry

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.300
collaborators, and even
competitors to work together.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.830
So there at this point I think
five of the top cloud companies

00:15:49.830 --> 00:15:52.050
are all members of this,
and getting together

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:53.510
and working on this.

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:57.815
So how do projects get selected
or participate in the Cloud

00:15:57.815 --> 00:15:59.100
Native Compute Foundation?

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.080
If your project is interesting
and relates to this cloud

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:05.490
nativeness there is a
technical oversight committee

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:08.970
that is independent of the
governing board and independent

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:11.700
of the corporate
contributions that

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.730
is setup to help guide
the projects that

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:17.530
participate in the Cloud
Native Compute Foundation.

00:16:17.530 --> 00:16:22.020
So if you have a project,
and you want to participate,

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:24.990
you basically go to the TOC,
and make a presentation,

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:26.010
and say, here's why.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:27.990
Here's what we would like to get
from the Cloud Native Compute

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:28.650
Foundation.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:30.390
It might be marketing support.

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:33.550
It might be integration help.

00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.562
It might be CICD help.

00:16:35.562 --> 00:16:37.020
The Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:39.295
offers a lot of
services to the project.

00:16:39.295 --> 00:16:41.670
And then so you say what you'd
like from the Cloud Native

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:42.510
Compute Foundation.

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.259
And you say, here's
where your project is,

00:16:44.259 --> 00:16:47.010
and here's what the intent
is, and the goal, and here

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.265
are some users.

00:16:48.265 --> 00:16:49.890
And there are different
levels at which

00:16:49.890 --> 00:16:52.890
you can join or participate
in the Cloud Native Compute

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:55.440
Foundation when you
bring a project to it.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.052
But the TOC has to agree.

00:16:57.052 --> 00:16:59.010
MARK MANDEL: How do we
decide at a Google Cloud

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:00.910
level what goes to CNCF or not?

00:17:00.910 --> 00:17:02.430
SARAH NOVOTNY: So
as it turns out,

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.182
that's a slightly
messy question.

00:17:04.182 --> 00:17:05.640
MARK MANDEL: That's
why I asked it.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:06.900
SARAH NOVOTNY: I know.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:10.410
And there has been a new
policy created around this.

00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.859
But generally, the idea
is we take a look at what

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:16.500
the goals are for the project.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:20.730
And we see if that aligns with
it moving to a foundation.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:24.640
So some of the projects,
[? Isdio, ?] as an example,

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.150
another open source
project here, we're working

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.280
on that with IBM very closely.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:33.210
And we're working with the
intent to move it to the CNCF.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:38.190
But it doesn't, at the
moment, have as many end users

00:17:38.190 --> 00:17:41.760
as we would want to come
in as an incubated project

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.440
at the Cloud Native
Compute Foundation.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:44.640
So we're growing that.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:47.430
And we're working through
getting a solid governance

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:51.180
model down, and then potentially
moving it to the Cloud Native

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:52.440
Compute Foundation.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.480
But it really is
alignment of goals,

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:59.730
and whether or not we want to
setup governance that would

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:01.760
meet the CNCF's requirements.

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:03.090
They're fairly limited, but.

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:05.640
MELANIE WARRICK: So I think
this is very interesting.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.830
You've talked about
the independence.

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:10.830
How do you make sure
that it is independent,

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:14.280
that Google is involved, and
other cloud providers are

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:18.120
involved, and yet they are not
directing projects and having

00:18:18.120 --> 00:18:19.980
influence over projects
that are trying

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:22.470
to come in that are not
from these organizations,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:23.565
or even are from?

00:18:23.565 --> 00:18:27.100
SARAH NOVOTNY: So, governance.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:29.347
Governance is the
topic of the week.

00:18:29.347 --> 00:18:30.930
So the Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.907
was actually built with
that model in mind.

00:18:33.907 --> 00:18:35.490
So that's one of the
reasons we didn't

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:36.810
go to the Apache Foundation.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:38.310
That's one of the reasons
we didn't go straight

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.310
to the Linux Foundation.

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.190
We wanted to build
our own little model

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:44.160
and our own little government
in order to make sure

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:45.780
that we had that balance.

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:49.170
And love him or not, Craig
McLuckie set this up.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:53.550
And he built this
in a way that gives

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:56.010
sort of checks and
balances, much like the US

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.217
governmental system.

00:18:57.217 --> 00:18:58.050
MELANIE WARRICK: Ah.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:01.410
SARAH NOVOTNY: And so the
governing board is very much

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.450
a corporate membership board.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:05.700
And it primarily
controls budget.

00:19:05.700 --> 00:19:08.010
It controls some
of the marketing.

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:10.230
It controls how
people are engaging.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.780
It runs the events.

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:14.670
But all of the
project interactions

00:19:14.670 --> 00:19:17.760
end up happening with
governance pushed very much down

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:19.480
to the projects themselves.

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:23.010
And I started to say there's
very light requirements

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.600
around governance from the CNCF.

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:27.210
The requirements are it just has
to be really well documented.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:30.570
And we really don't prefer
benevolent dictators.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:32.160
It's just, we want
to see something

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.201
that's broader than that.

00:19:33.201 --> 00:19:35.970
And we want to see a way
for a first time contributor

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:38.160
to be able to grow to
be a maintainer someday.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:42.330
And so there's the governing
board, the technical oversight

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:44.290
committee, and an
end user board.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:46.740
So these are the three branches
of Cloud Native Compute

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:47.340
Governance.

00:19:47.340 --> 00:19:49.890
And the technical
oversight committee

00:19:49.890 --> 00:19:52.500
has nine members
total, seven of whom

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.670
are appointed by the governing
board, who are not necessarily

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:59.370
supposed to be corporate
representatives,

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.290
but are industry
representatives.

00:20:01.290 --> 00:20:04.740
So we have people that sit
on the technical oversight

00:20:04.740 --> 00:20:07.620
board that don't have
governing board seats,

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:09.900
or their companies don't
have governing board seats,

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:13.470
because they are
representative of the industry

00:20:13.470 --> 00:20:16.410
and the cloud native
view of the world.

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:19.650
Then there are two technical
oversight committee seats

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:21.625
that are selected by
the technical oversight

00:20:21.625 --> 00:20:22.500
committee themselves.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.260
They say, let's look at the
seven of us hanging out here.

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.685
And we say we're
missing this skill.

00:20:27.685 --> 00:20:30.060
And we really need someone to
be able to talk about that.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:32.850
So they select people
from the industry as well.

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:35.310
And that is the
group that ends up

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:39.000
having project level interest
for bringing projects in

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.630
or graduating projects.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:43.237
Kubernetes was recently the
first graduated project.

00:20:43.237 --> 00:20:44.820
So we're now an
official full project.

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:45.540
MARK MANDEL: Woo.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:46.320
MELANIE WARRICK: Congrats.

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:47.250
SARAH NOVOTNY: Thank you.

00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:47.749
Yeah.

00:20:47.749 --> 00:20:49.950
That was back in March.

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:53.260
So the technical oversight
committee has that power.

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:56.550
They also have, I believe, in
their charter, a way to say,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.830
if there's not a lot
of progress, I'm sorry.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:20:59.850
You haven't graduated.

00:20:59.850 --> 00:21:03.190
You haven't made enough
progress in some amount of time.

00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:06.064
And so we need to move you on.

00:21:06.064 --> 00:21:08.230
MARK MANDEL: Put you on a
personal improvement plan.

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:08.450
SARAH NOVOTNY: Right.

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:09.670
A Project Improvement Plan.

00:21:09.670 --> 00:21:10.170
It's a PIP.

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:10.240
MARK MANDEL: There we go.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:10.972
It's a PIP.

00:21:10.972 --> 00:21:12.430
SARAH NOVOTNY: So
that's one group.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:15.870
And then the end user
board also gets one member

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.420
that gets to be sat on the
technical oversight committee.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:22.030
So there is also sort of a
perspective from an end user.

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:25.470
And so we tried to build
this balanced system.

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.570
It'll just be broken in
a new and different way,

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:29.945
because every system is.

00:21:29.945 --> 00:21:30.820
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:21:30.820 --> 00:21:33.236
SARAH NOVOTNY: But we thought
this one through pretty far,

00:21:33.236 --> 00:21:35.020
trying to make it more balanced.

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:36.090
MELANIE WARRICK: So
no gerrymandering.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:36.910
SARAH NOVOTNY:
Here's hoping not.

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:37.660
MELANIE WARRICK:
Here's hoping not.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:39.840
So if somebody is
building out a project

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:44.850
and wants to open source and
look at submitting to CNCF,

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:47.250
what would be approaches
you'd recommend,

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:50.826
or resources you'd recommend
for them to consider?

00:21:50.826 --> 00:21:52.950
SARAH NOVOTNY: Starting a
project is no small feat.

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:54.270
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, let's
say they've started one.

00:21:54.270 --> 00:21:54.560
SARAH NOVOTNY: OK.

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:55.060
Phew.

00:21:55.060 --> 00:21:56.208
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:56.208 --> 00:21:57.810
MELANIE WARRICK: Let's say
they've already started it.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:21:58.620
They've got something.

00:21:58.620 --> 00:21:58.980
SARAH NOVOTNY: Right.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:21:59.872
There are licenses.

00:21:59.872 --> 00:22:01.330
There's a little
bit of governance.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:02.790
We've got some code of conduct.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.260
We're doing good things.

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:04.780
OK.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:05.280
Yeah.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:06.950
We maybe got some end users.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:07.150
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:07.560
MARK MANDEL: Maybe.

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:09.330
SARAH NOVOTNY: Contributors
across different companies.

00:22:09.330 --> 00:22:09.810
MARK MANDEL: Sure.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:10.490
SARAH NOVOTNY: All right.

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:11.156
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:11.156 --> 00:22:12.670
SARAH NOVOTNY: Fantastic.

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:14.335
I just skipped all
the hard parts.

00:22:14.335 --> 00:22:16.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, and
let's say they're independent,

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:17.965
and/ or their company.

00:22:17.965 --> 00:22:19.590
Their company is
looking at doing this,

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.240
and they're trying to
figure out where to go.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.906
SARAH NOVOTNY: I,
again, go back to what

00:22:22.906 --> 00:22:25.600
is the strategic
goal of that project.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:29.520
So if a company is trying to
make an open source project,

00:22:29.520 --> 00:22:31.770
my suspicion is that
their strategic goal

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:34.227
is to get lots of
people excited about

00:22:34.227 --> 00:22:36.060
and interested in the
project, and then they

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:39.030
have a way to monetize
it in some magical form.

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:42.060
And if that's the
case, then there's

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:45.690
a sort of rising tides lifts
all ships method in saying,

00:22:45.690 --> 00:22:47.880
maybe Cloud Native
Compute Foundation, which

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.797
is really exciting, it
seems to fit with this.

00:22:49.797 --> 00:22:50.296
Yeah.

00:22:50.296 --> 00:22:52.200
We're totally down with
container packaged.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:54.480
Oh, look microservices oriented.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:55.270
All right.

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:56.520
Do we have dynamic scheduling?

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:58.167
Do we have good telemetry?

00:22:58.167 --> 00:22:59.250
Can we play in this space?

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.450
Can we add value in this space?

00:23:01.450 --> 00:23:04.350
And then if it fits with
those particular tenants,

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:08.940
and it seems to be a good
space and good technological

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:11.850
integration point with the
different other projects,

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.940
it's worth taking it to
the TOC and offering it.

00:23:14.940 --> 00:23:18.870
But it really always goes back
to what is their goal for me.

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:22.170
Like if what they really want
to do is just be super exciting,

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.770
hey, get friends to
put it on Hacker News.

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:26.137
That'll get you further faster.

00:23:26.137 --> 00:23:30.690
But if you want to be part of
a growing ecosystem in order

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.761
to draw in more
enterprise customers,

00:23:32.761 --> 00:23:34.260
then Cloud Native
Compute Foundation

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:36.090
might be the right place.

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:38.340
If you want to show
up and have someone

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:40.890
say, here is
prescriptive governance,

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:44.040
and here's a whole bunch of
stuff that we can offer you,

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.920
Apache might be
the right choice.

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:49.650
There are lots and lots
of ways to do open source,

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:51.399
and open source within
foundations even.

00:23:51.399 --> 00:23:53.440
MARK MANDEL: So now I'm
actually kind of curious.

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:55.564
What would be like, I don't
want to say the biggest

00:23:55.564 --> 00:23:58.410
mistake, but maybe the biggest
mistake that you've seen,

00:23:58.410 --> 00:24:01.710
or you can imagine that an
open source project could do

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:03.900
in this environment,
either to prevent them

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:07.170
from becoming largely adopted,
or access to contributors,

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:08.340
that kind of stuff?

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:10.530
What sort of challenges,
is maybe a better way

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:11.670
of putting it then.

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:13.295
SARAH NOVOTNY: There
are a couple right

00:24:13.295 --> 00:24:17.000
now that are pretty common
and getting less acceptable,

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:20.550
which is not being
welcoming to people who

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:23.040
are new, not being inclusive.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:28.200
Not allowing and accepting
that diverse voices,

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:31.470
even diverse skill set
voices can bring value

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:32.470
to your project.

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:34.410
20 years ago in
open source it was

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:38.310
perfectly OK to drum someone
out for asking a dumb question,

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:40.190
drum you off the
mailing list because you

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:41.670
asked something dumb.

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:43.800
Or answer RTFM.

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.140
And that's no longer
acceptable in any way.

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:48.810
And that will get you a
pretty bad rap in open source

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:49.580
right now.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:51.538
Which is not to say there
aren't still projects

00:24:51.538 --> 00:24:53.370
that run this way.

00:24:53.370 --> 00:24:56.460
But they are known,
and they are not

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.815
what I would call the
forward motion way.

00:24:58.815 --> 00:25:00.690
MARK MANDEL: So that is
actually interesting.

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:03.115
You say in 20 years
open source has changed.

00:25:03.115 --> 00:25:04.740
I've got some opinions
on this, but I'd

00:25:04.740 --> 00:25:07.830
love to hear yours on how
have you seen open source

00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:09.090
change over the last 20 years?

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.610
It does seem like the landscape
has shifted quite a lot.

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:13.530
SARAH NOVOTNY: Well,
in 2010 we said

00:25:13.530 --> 00:25:16.570
that open source had won, which
I thought was kind of awesome.

00:25:16.570 --> 00:25:19.160
So I was also program chair
for OSCON for five years.

00:25:19.160 --> 00:25:22.770
And so I did a lot of broad,
open source messaging,

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:26.860
and crossed paths with lots
and lots of projects that way.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:31.270
And the tagline in, I think
it was the 2010 OSCON, was,

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:32.450
open source has won.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:33.360
OK.

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:36.820
It's now the default
way to build languages.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:39.070
It's now the default
way to build large scale

00:25:39.070 --> 00:25:40.930
infrastructure projects.

00:25:40.930 --> 00:25:43.660
You don't try to build
proprietary stacks so much

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:44.450
anymore.

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:46.658
The big shift that I saw,
which would have been right

00:25:46.658 --> 00:25:50.080
around that time was
when MySQL, and Oracle,

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:54.160
and all of the proliferated
no SQL databases showed up.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:57.670
They were all open
source, almost all.

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.040
Or OpenStack coming
in and saying,

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.360
no, we're doing open
source a new way.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:04.330
We're doing it with
lots of companies.

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:08.020
We're doing it with lots of
big tent view of bringing

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:09.370
in lots and lots of projects.

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:11.426
We can argue all
week whether that

00:26:11.426 --> 00:26:12.550
was a success or a failure.

00:26:12.550 --> 00:26:14.260
But it was a new
way of doing it.

00:26:14.260 --> 00:26:18.340
And it was a new default
where we saw people say,

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:23.680
we have a problem, and the
industry and the vendors

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:24.770
aren't solving it.

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:27.346
So we need to find a way
to fix this ourselves.

00:26:27.346 --> 00:26:28.720
MARK MANDEL: It
also feels to me,

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:30.678
and I'd love to hear your
opinion on this, that

00:26:30.678 --> 00:26:33.100
back 15 years ago, if there
was an open source project,

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:34.330
it was run by one person.

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:36.250
And everyone was
like, that's fine.

00:26:36.250 --> 00:26:37.119
No problem.

00:26:37.119 --> 00:26:39.160
Whereas now it seems like
an open source project,

00:26:39.160 --> 00:26:41.722
if people come to it, if
it's not company backed,

00:26:41.722 --> 00:26:43.180
or there's not a
large organization

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:45.100
of some kind behind it,
then it's not nearly as well

00:26:45.100 --> 00:26:46.780
received I guess
would be the word.

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:48.654
Have you seen that sort
of happening as well,

00:26:48.654 --> 00:26:49.720
or have opinions on that?

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:51.910
SARAH NOVOTNY: I have
lots of opinions on that.

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:53.980
I don't know that it's
not as well received.

00:26:53.980 --> 00:26:57.580
But I think in the same way
that we have spent the last 10

00:26:57.580 --> 00:27:00.220
years learning from
infrastructure having

00:27:00.220 --> 00:27:02.290
single points of
failure, projects

00:27:02.290 --> 00:27:05.380
having single points of
failure is a huge problem.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:08.710
There was a great article, and
it's still probably a problem.

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:10.360
I'm not 100% sure.

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:15.790
The man who supports and
builds NTP is like one guy.

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:20.160
His source code is on his
desktop underneath his desk.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.870
It's in an old
revision control system

00:27:22.870 --> 00:27:26.860
that he's super familiar
with, but nobody else really

00:27:26.860 --> 00:27:28.560
wants to get as involved with.

00:27:28.560 --> 00:27:32.590
And so these are problems.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:39.071
And we run our infrastructure
on this open source project.

00:27:39.071 --> 00:27:41.320
So there was a lot of work
done over the last 10 years

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:44.650
to try to make those
projects more robust.

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:47.390
And it still is a huge problem.

00:27:47.390 --> 00:27:48.940
We have libraries
that are completely

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:53.350
unmaintained in open source that
people use, and incorporate,

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:57.244
and include in everything.

00:27:57.244 --> 00:27:58.160
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:00.360
That's definitely a challenge.

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:03.670
What's interesting to me
from working in open source

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:05.680
is understanding the fact
that even though it's

00:28:05.680 --> 00:28:08.530
company backed, doesn't
always necessarily mean it's

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:10.630
going to be as well-maintained.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:11.380
SARAH NOVOTNY: No.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:12.296
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:28:12.296 --> 00:28:17.020
I've seen both sides, from
individual parties to companies

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:20.440
being really great at it, as
well as also really challenged.

00:28:20.440 --> 00:28:22.480
You've talked about
some of the challenges.

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:25.520
Where do you think open
source is going to go?

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:29.080
How do you think this is going
to look so many years down

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.624
the line from what
you're seeing right now?

00:28:31.624 --> 00:28:33.040
SARAH NOVOTNY: I
think we're going

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:37.250
to continue to see large
scale corporate investment.

00:28:37.250 --> 00:28:41.890
I think we will start to get
some more evolved templates

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:45.350
for new projects, evolved
templates for governance.

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:49.780
So it's not quite so much
making it up new every time.

00:28:49.780 --> 00:28:51.700
As I said, there was
that big inflection point

00:28:51.700 --> 00:28:53.740
from sort of
individual contributors

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:56.820
to corporate contributors
long about 2010 or '12.

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:59.620
We're still making
things up though, now,

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:01.190
even five years on.

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:04.150
And as I said, we make
new mistakes each time,

00:29:04.150 --> 00:29:06.280
which is a great thing to
do, except for the fact

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:08.080
that it would be
great to just fix

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:09.730
some of the mistakes
in the other ones.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:12.990
But 5 years, 10
years from now, I

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:17.830
think that we will see
open source be the way.

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:20.830
We develop a lot of the
shared infrastructure.

00:29:20.830 --> 00:29:23.380
I think we're seeing
already that, as opposed

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:26.120
to the standards bodies
of the '80s and '90s,

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:28.930
we pull together around
an open source reference

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:31.450
implementation, and
then sort of write

00:29:31.450 --> 00:29:33.300
a specification around that.

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.160
And then that becomes the
de facto industry standard.

00:29:36.160 --> 00:29:38.590
The open container initiative
is a good example of this.

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:40.150
Docker did a thing.

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:41.660
It was based on containers.

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:42.550
This is super cool.

00:29:42.550 --> 00:29:44.650
Containers had been
in Linux for years.

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:48.126
But Docker really did a great
job of making it accessible.

00:29:48.126 --> 00:29:49.750
Then people got
worried that maybe they

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:51.220
had too much control.

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:53.050
But it was still open source.

00:29:53.050 --> 00:29:56.710
So why don't we try to figure
out what the specification is,

00:29:56.710 --> 00:30:00.130
and then make a reference
implementation that

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:04.720
is cross compatible with Docker,
and then compare that, and then

00:30:04.720 --> 00:30:07.210
setup conformance tests,
and understand what

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:09.610
we mean by a container runtime.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.860
What do we mean by a
container image specification?

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:13.460
Write all that out.

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:15.610
And this all happened
without a single standards

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:16.522
body being involved.

00:30:16.522 --> 00:30:18.480
And so I think we're
going to see the standards

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:22.650
bodies continue to exist
and be used, because they're

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:26.490
super useful for especially
highly regulated industries.

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:29.310
But I think they will be a
second stop for open source

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:31.650
reference implementations
and specifications.

00:30:31.650 --> 00:30:33.650
So I think that's going
to be one of the biggest

00:30:33.650 --> 00:30:36.554
changes is that we do a lot
more in open source around that.

00:30:36.554 --> 00:30:37.470
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:40.500
MARK MANDEL: And just to go
in a slightly different tack,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.570
I've definitely run into
people, or corporations,

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:45.919
I should say possibly, who
when approached or discussing

00:30:45.919 --> 00:30:48.210
the idea of open source, it
seems very foreign to them.

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:49.680
If you're a company,
and potentially you're

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:51.270
either looking at open
source, or even just wondering

00:30:51.270 --> 00:30:52.590
why people would do
this, what do you

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:54.660
feel like the motivations
are for large companies

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:56.559
to really start going
down this trend?

00:30:56.559 --> 00:30:58.350
And what are the
benefits, I guess, really,

00:30:58.350 --> 00:30:59.680
at the end of the day?

00:30:59.680 --> 00:31:02.980
SARAH NOVOTNY: There's a lot of
economic benefit to a platform.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:05.220
And if you open
your platform, you

00:31:05.220 --> 00:31:07.170
have more people able
to contribute to it,

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:09.250
which then gets you
a network effect.

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:11.730
So almost anything
that can be looked

00:31:11.730 --> 00:31:17.190
at as a pluggable kernel,
for lack of a better term,

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:20.100
can benefit from being
open sourced and grown.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:22.560
And then the ecosystem,
the addressable market

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.246
of whatever you're doing,
gets bigger because you

00:31:25.246 --> 00:31:26.370
have a community around it.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.366
And you have people that
are excited about it.

00:31:28.366 --> 00:31:30.360
You have people advocating
for that platform,

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.550
advocating for
that new paradigm.

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:36.980
And then you have more
business opportunity.

00:31:36.980 --> 00:31:40.090
So there's certainly
altruistic motives.

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:43.470
It's better for, many
eyes make shallow bugs.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:45.480
There's lots of good reasons.

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:47.580
But ultimately, we
still live in a world

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:49.380
where this is commercial.

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:53.910
And in order to make
this viable for a company

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.670
to support, and
make it viable long

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:59.340
term sustainable, by
having multiple companies

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.740
and individuals come together
and build these sometimes

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:05.480
enormous projects, you need to
have business value come out

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:05.980
of it.

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:07.822
So there are good
economic reasons to do it.

00:32:07.822 --> 00:32:09.030
