00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:48.010
Mark, question of the week.

00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:51.120
You broke your SSH access to
your compute engine instance

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:54.391
by accidentally removing the
GCP Linux guest environment.

00:34:54.391 --> 00:34:55.057
[GLASS SMASHING]

00:34:55.057 --> 00:34:56.429
[DIGITAL BLOOPS]

00:34:56.429 --> 00:34:59.160
This reminds me of a time when
I think I did-- what was it?

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:00.484
Sudo--

00:35:00.484 --> 00:35:01.400
MARK MANDEL: Like RMR?

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:03.990
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:35:03.990 --> 00:35:05.040
So how did you fix this?

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:05.310
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:07.434
So first of all, I want to
say, you should probably

00:35:07.434 --> 00:35:08.910
automate all your build things.

00:35:08.910 --> 00:35:13.020
Use containers, or some sort of
automation script, [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:14.700
So in this case I
wasn't doing this.

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:17.030
That's probably
mistake number one.

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:18.810
But I had a GC instance.

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:21.080
It's running WordPress on
it so I can run my blog.

00:35:21.080 --> 00:35:24.050
And I was like, oh, a new
version of Debian's out.

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.610
I should upgrade
because security.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:26.660
That's good.

00:35:26.660 --> 00:35:27.920
So I sat down.

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:29.520
I went through the
steps, upgraded.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:30.560
Everything was fine.

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:33.230
What I didn't realize was
in doing said upgrade,

00:35:33.230 --> 00:35:35.150
there's a bunch of
GCP specific tooling

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:37.940
that handles all the SSH key
and security stuff for when

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:38.780
you want to SSH in.

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:40.760
So you can SSH in from
your browser, for example,

00:35:40.760 --> 00:35:42.800
and/ or from the G Cloud
tools, and it handles all that.

00:35:42.800 --> 00:35:45.080
Unfortunately, when I
upgraded, that thing goes away.

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:46.587
Unless you manually
take the step

00:35:46.587 --> 00:35:48.170
to make sure that
tool is still there,

00:35:48.170 --> 00:35:49.880
and the repo is still there,
and all that kind of stuff,

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:50.900
it just disappears.

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:55.220
So I was stuck with a machine
that I couldn't SSH into.

00:35:55.220 --> 00:35:57.290
It was still working, but
I couldn't SSH into it.

00:35:57.290 --> 00:35:57.789
Anyway.

00:35:57.789 --> 00:36:00.140
So there's actually
a really good guide,

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:02.390
that we'll link to in the
show notes, that is actually

00:36:02.390 --> 00:36:05.390
for if you're setting
up your own OS image,

00:36:05.390 --> 00:36:06.462
or something like that.

00:36:06.462 --> 00:36:08.420
But it is linked from
the troubleshooting guide

00:36:08.420 --> 00:36:12.590
if you can't SSH in, talking
about how you can install

00:36:12.590 --> 00:36:14.750
the tooling without
actually having

00:36:14.750 --> 00:36:16.710
to be able to SSH into the box.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:19.220
So they take you through steps
where you clone the disk,

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:22.280
fire up another VM,
mount and setup a setup

00:36:22.280 --> 00:36:24.350
script that you can be
copy pasted into it,

00:36:24.350 --> 00:36:25.100
fire up the thing.

00:36:25.100 --> 00:36:26.420
And then basically
the machine loads up,

00:36:26.420 --> 00:36:28.370
and then it installs the stuff
that it needs to install,

00:36:28.370 --> 00:36:29.690
and it continues on from there.

00:36:29.690 --> 00:36:30.380
And it works.

00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:31.850
I can SSH back into my box.

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:32.770
MELANIE WARRICK:
Congratulations.

00:36:32.770 --> 00:36:33.705
MARK MANDEL: So I will link it.

00:36:33.705 --> 00:36:35.512
It's one of those
things that you probably

00:36:35.512 --> 00:36:37.220
